---
import { renderToString } from "katex";
import EvidenceDetails from "./EvidenceDetails.astro";
import GravesFormulationPrerequisite from "./GravesFormulationPrerequisite.astro";
import JourneyMap from "./JourneyMap.astro";
import ModeSwitch from "./ModeSwitch.astro";
import SourceReversalLedger from "./SourceReversalLedger.astro";
import { getJourneyOverview, type StageView } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { view: StageView }
const { view } = Astro.props;
const { mode } = view;
const expert = mode === "expert";
if (view.sourceReversal === undefined) throw new Error("Sutskever needs its reviewed source-reversal example.");
const journey = getJourneyOverview("encoder-decoder-alignment");
const claim = (id: string) => {
  const evidence = view.evidence.find((item) => item.claimId === `sutskever-2014-${id}`);
  if (evidence === undefined) throw new Error(`Missing Sutskever evidence: ${id}`);
  return evidence;
};
const math = (value: string) => renderToString(value, { displayMode: true, output: "htmlAndMathml", throwOnError: true });
const direct = [
  ["Bahdanau et al. · comparison system", "28.45"],
  ["Phrase-based baseline · comparison system", "33.30"],
  ["1 forward LSTM · beam 12", "26.17"],
  ["1 reversed LSTM · beam 12", "30.59"],
  ["5 reversed LSTMs · beam 1", "33.00"],
  ["2 reversed LSTMs · beam 12", "33.27"],
  ["5 reversed LSTMs · beam 2", "34.50"],
  ["5 reversed LSTMs · beam 12", "34.81"],
];
const rescoring = [
  ["Phrase-based baseline", "33.30"],
  ["Cho et al. · phrase-based combination", "34.54"],
  ["Comparison system · re-evaluated consistently", "37.0"],
  ["Baseline 1,000-best + 1 forward LSTM", "35.61"],
  ["Baseline 1,000-best + 1 reversed LSTM", "35.85"],
  ["Baseline 1,000-best + 5 reversed LSTMs", "36.5"],
  ["Baseline 1,000-best oracle · reference-informed", "≈45"],
];
---
<article class="stage-shell alignment-stage">
  <div class="stage-reading">
    <ModeSwitch activeMode={mode} alternateHref={view.alternateMode.href} />
    <header class="stage-header"><p class="stage-number">Stage 2 of 4 · first preprint September 10, 2014 · NIPS oral December 9</p><h1>{expert ? "Which source order, search and result pipeline?" : "What changes when we reverse the source?"}</h1><p class="stage-question">{view.contribution}</p><dl class="stage-meta"><div><dt>Work</dt><dd>{view.work.title} · Sutskever, Vinyals and Le</dd></div><div><dt>Display source</dt><dd>{view.canonicalVersion.label}</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Deep LSTM · fixed source summary · direct decoding and rescoring</dd></div></dl></header>
    <section class="objective-band" aria-labelledby="objective-heading"><h2 id="objective-heading">Your task</h2><p>{view.objective.objective}</p><details><summary>Success criterion</summary><p>{view.objective.successCriterion}</p></details></section>
    <nav class="preset-index mt-4" aria-label="Sutskever chapter sections"><a href="#graves-formulation-prerequisite">LSTM prerequisite</a><a href="#source-reversal-heading">Source order</a><a href="#sutskever-pipelines">Two pipelines</a><a href="#sutskever-direct-results">Paper results</a></nav>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="sutskever-source-heading"><p class="kicker">Architecture · initialize from the source</p><h2 id="sutskever-source-heading">Read the source, then generate the target.</h2><p>One deep long short-term memory network (LSTM) encodes the source into a fixed-width vector v. That vector initializes a separate deep LSTM language model, which predicts target words from the target prefix and ends with EOS. Both networks have four layers of 1,000 cells. Source and target lengths may differ.</p>{!expert && <p class="mt-3">The target prefix is the words already produced. EOS is the end-of-sequence symbol: predicting it ends that candidate translation. The summary has a fixed number of coordinates even when the source has more words.</p>}<p class="mt-3">In Cho’s evaluated system, the neural model scores phrase-table pairs. Here a decoder can propose an entire translation directly. Both use one source summary; there is no new read over individual source positions at every target step.</p>
      {expert && <><div class="equation-panel" role="region" aria-label="Sutskever conditional sequence likelihood and decoding objective" tabindex="0"><div set:html={math(String.raw`p(y\mid x)=\prod_{t=1}^{T'}p(y_t\mid v,y_{<t}),\quad v=\mathrm{Encoder}(x)`)} /><div set:html={math(String.raw`\max_\theta\frac{1}{|\mathcal S|}\sum_{(x,y)\in\mathcal S}\log p_\theta(y\mid x),\quad \widehat y\approx\arg\max_y p_\theta(y\mid x)`)} /></div><p>The first expression follows Eq. 1, with a vocabulary softmax at every step and T′ including target EOS. The second separates the §3.2 training objective from approximate Eq. 2 search. Training scores observed prefixes; generation extends model-produced prefixes. The text calls v the last hidden state; it does not specify a modern framework’s hidden/cell-state tuple.</p></>}
      <EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("fixed-vector-factorization")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("deep-two-lstm-architecture")} />
    </section>
    <GravesFormulationPrerequisite view={view} />
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="sutskever-reversal-evidence"><p class="kicker">Measured intervention · proposed explanation</p><h2 id="sutskever-reversal-evidence">A useful reversal, with an incomplete account.</h2><p>Only source word order reverses. Section 3.3 reports test perplexity falling from 5.8 to 4.7 and decoded BLEU rising from 25.9 to 30.6. Table 1 instead prints 26.17 and 30.59 for its beam-12 single-model rows. The paper does not reconcile the forward-score difference.</p>{!expert && <p class="mt-3">Perplexity summarizes how surprised the model is by reference words; lower is better. BLEU evaluates the produced translations against references; higher is better under the same evaluation procedure. They answer different questions.</p>}<p class="mt-3">The authors propose that shorter early source–target gaps make optimization easier while average aligned-word distance stays unchanged. They explicitly describe the explanation as incomplete. The synthetic ledger illustrates early, late and mean distance under declared pairs; it measures no gradients and supplies no causal test.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("reversal-intervention")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("reversal-explanation-boundary")} /></section>
  </div>
  <aside class="stage-workbench stage-workbench--long alignment-workbench" aria-label="Static source-reversal comparison"><SourceReversalLedger scenario={view.sourceReversal} mode={mode} /></aside>
  <div class="alignment-evidence">
    <section class="reading-block alignment-result-section" aria-labelledby="sutskever-pipelines"><p class="kicker">Candidate origin · keep the tasks separate</p><h2 id="sutskever-pipelines">Generate a sentence or rerank existing sentences.</h2>
      <p>Beam width B is the number of unfinished candidate prefixes retained at each step. An ensemble combines several models; its model count is separate from the beam width.</p>
      <div class="pipeline-comparison"><section><h3>Direct generation</h3><ol class="copy-path" aria-label="Direct neural generation path"><li><strong>Encode</strong><span>Build v from the reversed source.</span></li><li><strong>Expand prefixes</strong><span>Try target-vocabulary continuations; retain B live hypotheses by model log probability.</span></li><li><strong>Finish at EOS</strong><span>Move completed hypotheses aside. A finite beam approximates the most likely translation.</span></li></ol><p class="mt-3">The headline 34.81 uses five reversed-source LSTMs and beam 12. The phrase-based baseline supplies no candidate list to this decoding path.</p></section><section><h3>1,000-best rescoring</h3><ol class="copy-path" aria-label="Phrase-based list rescoring path"><li><strong>Receive candidates</strong><span>The phrase-based baseline supplies up to 1,000 candidate translations per source.</span></li><li><strong>Score each candidate</strong><span>Compute its LSTM log probability; average it evenly with the baseline’s score.</span></li><li><strong>Rerank the list</strong><span>Select among supplied candidates. This path cannot generate a missing candidate.</span></li></ol><p class="mt-3">The headline 36.5 uses five reversed-source LSTMs plus this phrase-based list. It is a hybrid system result.</p></section></div>
      {expert && <p class="provenance-note mt-4">§3.2 specifies log-probability beam ranking and an even average for rescoring; it specifies no length normalization. B counts live prefixes, not models. The ensemble members differ in initialization and minibatch order; these passages do not specify the ensemble probability-combination rule, so no such rule is calculated here.</p>}
      <details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>Check the candidate source behind 36.5</summary><p>The phrase-based baseline supplies the candidate list. Neural scores reorder that list; they do not turn it into direct generation. The ≈45 oracle uses reference knowledge to describe opportunity within the list, not an attainable decoder result.</p></details><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("beam-and-rescoring")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block alignment-result-section" aria-labelledby="sutskever-direct-results"><p class="kicker">Canonical Table 1 · article pp. 5–6</p><h2 id="sutskever-direct-results">Read source order, ensemble and beam together.</h2><p>All scores below are test BLEU on WMT’14 English–French newstest2014 (ntst14). The paper uses cased, tokenized predictions and references with multi-bleu.pl. BLEU is a translation evaluation score, not a word probability. The first two rows are comparison systems; the remaining rows decode from LSTMs.</p><table class="trace-table sutskever-results mt-4"><caption>Canonical Table 1 · complete direct-decoding comparison</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">System and search conditions</th><th scope="col">Test BLEU</th></tr></thead><tbody>{direct.map(([label, value]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td>{value}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table><p class="mt-3">The five-model beam-2 row reaches 34.50; beam 12 reaches 34.81. The paper describes five models at beam 2 as cheaper than one model at beam 12. This is its configuration-specific comparison, without a hardware-normalized cost benchmark.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("direct-results")} /></section>
    <section class="reading-block alignment-result-section" aria-labelledby="sutskever-rescoring-results"><p class="kicker">Canonical Table 2 · article p. 6</p><h2 id="sutskever-rescoring-results">Rescoring inherits the candidate list.</h2><table class="trace-table sutskever-results mt-4"><caption>Canonical Table 2 · complete SMT-combination and rescoring comparison</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">System and candidate conditions</th><th scope="col">Test BLEU</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rescoring.map(([label, value]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td>{value}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table><p class="mt-3">The 36.5 result improves the selected baseline by 3.2 BLEU points and remains 0.5 below the 37.0 comparison system. The latter is re-evaluated with the same tokenized script; its separately reported 35.8 uses a different evaluation convention. Cho’s 34.54 includes the conventional translator, neural phrase score, separate language model and word penalty.</p><p class="provenance-note mt-3">The oracle is reference-informed list selection. No beam size is specified for the rescoring rows: the target does not regenerate the baseline’s list. These cross-system rows are not matched component ablations, and no confidence intervals or significance tests are supplied.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("rescoring-results")} /></section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="sutskever-data-heading"><p class="kicker">Experimental envelope · article pp. 4–5</p><h2 id="sutskever-data-heading">A large model with a closed vocabulary.</h2><p>The selected training subset has 12 million sentence pairs: 304 million English words and 348 million French words. The source vocabulary retains 160,000 words, the target 80,000; all other words map to UNK. There is no subword or copying mechanism to recover a particular missing word.</p><p class="mt-3">The best direct score is penalized when reference words fall outside the target vocabulary. The reported model uses 1,000-dimensional word embeddings and an 80,000-way softmax.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("data-and-vocabulary")} />
      {expert && <><ul class="mt-3"><li>Proceedings: 380 million parameters; 64 million recurrent weights, split 32 million each between encoder and decoder.</li><li>Uniform initialization in [−0.08, 0.08]. Plain SGD without momentum, learning rate 0.7; halve every half epoch after epoch five; train for 7.5 epochs.</li><li>Batches of 128; divide gradients by batch size, then rescale the gradient vector to norm 5 when its norm exceeds 5. This differs from clipping each coordinate.</li><li>Length-grouped minibatches reduce padding waste. Reported speedup: roughly twofold.</li><li>Eight-GPU implementation: four GPUs for recurrent layers and four for softmax partitions. About 6,300 combined English/French words per second and ten days’ training; the single-GPU comparison reports about 1,700 words per second.</li></ul><p class="provenance-note mt-3">Nearly ten-percent perplexity reduction per added layer is a reported depth observation, without a matched-parameter ablation table. Runtime and throughput belong to this implementation; they are not portable scaling laws.</p></>}
      <EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("training-and-scale")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="sutskever-history-heading"><p class="kicker">Source versions · parallel development</p><h2 id="sutskever-history-heading">A teaching order is not an influence arrow.</h2><p>The September 10 first preprint explicitly describes parallel development with Cho and an independently taken fixed-vector strategy. The proceedings retains the comparison but omits that wording. The corpus therefore treats Cho–Sutskever as an editorial comparison with causal language prohibited.</p><p class="mt-3">Bahdanau’s first preprint is dated September 1, before Sutskever’s. This chapter comes first to teach the fixed-summary branch; chapter order establishes neither private development order nor sole priority.</p>
      <dl class="alignment-version-list"><div><dt>arXiv v1 · September 10, 2014</dt><dd>380 million parameters; explicit parallel-development wording; earlier evaluation-comparison text.</dd></div><div><dt>arXiv v2 · October 29, 2014</dt><dd>380 million parameters; revised common-script comparison; parallel wording omitted.</dd></div><div><dt>NIPS proceedings · 2014</dt><dd>Canonical PDF: 380 million parameters. The oral was December 9. This chapter uses its internal article pp. 1–9, not volume pp. 3104–3112.</dd></div><div><dt>arXiv v3 · December 14, 2014</dt><dd>384 million parameters and an 8,000-dimensional-state description. The four-million difference is unexplained; these details stay attached to v3.</dd></div></dl>
      {expert && <p class="provenance-note mt-3">The official metadata JSON embeds v3-like text while linking the 380-million proceedings PDF. The display source is the PDF; the two counts are not silently merged.</p>}
      <EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("cho-concurrency")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("version-parameter-boundary")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block alignment-boundaries" aria-labelledby="sutskever-limits-heading"><p class="kicker">Evidence boundary</p><h2 id="sutskever-limits-heading">Successful fixed summaries have bounded evidence.</h2><p>Figure 3 reports no BLEU decline below 35 words and only minor degradation in the longest displayed bin for this reversed-source ensemble. It has no numeric bin table, confidence intervals or matched unreversed curve. Selected long translations do not establish unbounded memory or guarantee success on other data.</p><p class="mt-3">Figure 2 projects a small selected phrase set with PCA. Its word-order and active/passive interpretation has no held-out semantic metric or systematic sampling protocol. Neither figure isolates the mechanism behind the gain.</p><p class="mt-3">The strongest results combine reversal, ensembling, a limited vocabulary and approximate search; rescoring also depends on the phrase-based list. The inspected versions supply no matched full component ablation, complete preprocessing implementation, released model or repeated-run uncertainty.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("long-sentence-boundary")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("representation-evidence")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("evidence-boundary")} /><a class="claim-link" href={`/reference/works/${view.work.id}/`}>Inspect the complete fifteen-claim Sutskever record</a></section>
    <nav class="reading-block alignment-progression" aria-label="Alignment journey progression"><p class="kicker">Next distinction · learned alignment</p><h2>What if the decoder could read source positions again?</h2><p>Continue with Bahdanau’s per-step source weighting over bidirectional annotations. Compare two supplied prior states, distinguish source weights from output-word probabilities, and inspect the separate Graves handwriting window.</p><p class="mt-3"><a class="button" href={`/journeys/encoder-decoder-alignment/${mode}/bahdanau-2014-2015/`}>Continue to Bahdanau in {mode} mode</a></p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button--quiet" href={`/journeys/encoder-decoder-alignment/${mode}/cho-2014/`}>Return to Cho in {mode} mode</a><a href={`/journeys/encoder-decoder-alignment/${mode}/`}>Return to the {mode} route map</a></div><div class="mt-4"><JourneyMap journey={journey} currentSlug={view.stage.slug} mode={mode} compact /></div></nav>
  </div>
</article>
